   "subject": "Re: [TMB users] Re: Trouble with syntax: How to pass to a vector a matrix * vector multiplication?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Tks Bob!<br><br>I solved it by doing element wise multiplication with arrays and summing then up, as you suggested:<br><div><br></div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">Z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">row</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">).</span><span style=\"color:#000\">array</span><span style=\"color:#660\">()*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">U</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">row</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">j</span><span style=\"color:#660\">).</span><span style=\"color:#000\">array</span><span style=\"color:#660\">()).</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sum</span><span style=\"color:#660\">()</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><br>On Monday, 4 May 2020 19:13:18 UTC-3, betasci007  wrote:</div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">HI Guilherme<div><br></div><div>There is lots of good guidance in the TMB book on matrix, array and vector operations. See the file matrix_array.cpp at</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/_book/matrix-arrays.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/_book/matrix-arrays.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792462161055000&amp;usg=AOvVaw11zFJNNJ5IKLw2rwTWDSei\">http://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/_book/matrix-arrays.html</a><br></div><div><br></div><div>There are a number of ways to do what you want. For matrix * vector multiplication, the general requirement is to ensure the appropriate inner dimensions are being used e.g. 5 x 3 matrix * 3 * 10 matrix produces 5 x 10 matrix. With vectors, if you have a 7 cell vector Z, using Z.matrix() converts this to a 7 x 1 matrix and with Z.matrix().transpose(), you create a 1 x 7 matrix. If you want to multiply each column of a matrix (m1) by a vector (v1), assuming the dimensions are correct, you can use the colwise() or rowwise() syntax as m1.array().colwise() * v1.array() or m1.array().rowwise() * v1.array() depending on whether or not you want to multiply (or divide) rows or columns. Note that the array() casting is necessary to indicate element by element multiplication. By chaining commands, you can sum the result e.g. type x = (m1.array().colwise() * v1.array()).sum(). </div><div><br></div><div>Hope this helps.</div><div><br></div><div>Bob </div><div><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"font-size:12.8px\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><br></div></div><br></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
