     "<div>Tks Chris for your alert! (I really thought that the multiplication\r\n that I did was inefficient). However, I didn&#39;t figured out how to do it\r\n in other way around.</div><div><br></div><div>I am modelling a variable which is Beta distributed and has this structure:</div><div><br></div><div><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/tmb-users/attach/7f94847bd818e/Auto%20Generated%20Inline%20Image%201?part=0.4&amp;view=1\" alt=\"\" width=\"200\" height=\"101\"></div><div><br></div><div>And\r\n for the random effect part structure, I would like to do the matrix \r\nmultiplication: Z*U, where Z is a n x 2 is the design matrix for random \r\neffect, and U is a n_id (number of individuals) x 2 is the parameter \r\nmatrix.</div><div><br></div><div>In this case, I need that &quot;a group of Z lines&quot; multiplies the respective transpose line of U:<br>Let&#39;s\r\n say that I have 10 individuals, and they were measured in 15 different \r\ntimes. So, the first 15 lines of Z have to multiply the first line of U \r\n(because is the random effect of the first individual); the 16-30 lines \r\nof Z have to multiply the transposed second line of U (r.e. of the 2nd \r\nindividual), and so on...</div><div><br></div><div>In this case, is there a way that I can use for use the multmat? (Via split, maybe?)<br></div><div><br></div><div>As far I understand I\r\n need to use the n_idx2 for the U matrix because I have 2 r.e. and I \r\nneed to pass U for a MVNORM_t&lt;Type&gt;<br></div><div><br></div><div>(I will attach the code that I have acomplished).<br></div><div><br></div>Thanks for your help"
